WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.054
From nineteen fifty-nine, British Rail ordered hundreds of locomotives powered by a single engine:

2
00:00:08.054 --> 00:00:10.605
the Sulzer twelve L D A twenty-eight.

3
00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:15.291
It hauled the Peaks, the Class forty-four, forty-five and forty-six,

4
00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:19.427
and the Brush Type Four, now known as the Class forty-seven.

5
00:00:19.427 --> 00:00:20.874
Here is how it works.

6
00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:24.397
Twelve cylinders, but not in a V.

7
00:00:24.397 --> 00:00:30.169
It is two separate inline sixes in one welded frame, each with its own crankshaft.

8
00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:35.237
Bore two hundred and eighty millimetres, stroke three hundred and sixty.

9
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:43.262
About twenty-two litres a cylinder, and roughly two hundred and sixty-six in all, calculated from bore and stroke.

10
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:48.611
The two crankshafts are tied together by synchronising gears,

11
00:00:48.611 --> 00:00:53.711
which step the speed up by one point four four to drive the main generator.

12
00:00:53.711 --> 00:00:56.771
The generator feeds electric traction motors.

13
00:00:56.771 --> 00:00:59.219
The engine never touches the wheels.

14
00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:02.942
Pull it apart and the layers appear.

15
00:01:02.942 --> 00:01:08.759
Crankcase, blocks and liners, heads with their valves, pistons and connecting rods,

16
00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:13.945
camshafts, injection pumps, and the turbochargers and intercoolers on top.

17
00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:19.833
Each cylinder works in four strokes over two turns of the crankshaft.

18
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:25.744
Intake: the piston falls and the inlet valve admits air, pushed in by the turbocharger.

19
00:01:25.744 --> 00:01:32.810
Compression: both valves shut, and the rising piston squeezes the air to a small fraction of its volume,

20
00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.207
heating it far above the ignition point of diesel.

21
00:01:36.207 --> 00:01:43.205
Power: fuel is injected just before top dead centre and burns, and the pressure drives the piston down.

22
00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:48.233
Exhaust: the exhaust valve opens, and the piston sweeps out the burnt gas.

23
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:54.429
The valve timing on this site is an engineering estimate, not Sulzer data.

24
00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:56.792
We could not find a primary source.

25
00:01:56.792 --> 00:02:02.935
Inlet and exhaust overlap around top dead centre, so that boost air can flush the cylinder.

26
00:02:04.135 --> 00:02:08.138
Twelve cylinders share seven hundred and twenty degrees,

27
00:02:08.138 --> 00:02:14.429
so on average a power stroke begins every sixty degrees, if the firing is evenly spaced.

28
00:02:14.429 --> 00:02:18.718
At full speed, seven hundred and fifty revolutions a minute,

29
00:02:18.718 --> 00:02:23.651
that is seventy-five firings every second: a seventy-five hertz note.

30
00:02:23.651 --> 00:02:26.510
The firing order shown is an assumption.

31
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.655
No source we reached gives it.

32
00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:32.940
Exhaust gas spins a de Havilland turbocharger,

33
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:39.645
which compresses the air to roughly two bar absolute on the Peaks, sixteen to eighteen pounds gauge.

34
00:02:39.645 --> 00:02:44.875
On the B and C engines, an intercooler cools that air, packing in more oxygen.

35
00:02:44.875 --> 00:02:51.512
The A, in the Class forty-four, had no intercooler, which is the likely reason it makes less power.

36
00:02:51.512 --> 00:02:56.675
That is an inference from the six-cylinder engine, not stated for the twelve.

37
00:02:57.875 --> 00:03:03.435
The sound comes from the same machinery: the thump of each firing, the exhaust puffs,

38
00:03:03.435 --> 00:03:09.191
the valve ticks, the turbocharger whine rising with load, and the hum of the gear train.

39
00:03:09.191 --> 00:03:14.686
The synthesiser on the site builds the note from those events, cylinder by cylinder.

40
00:03:15.886 --> 00:03:16.858
Three ratings.

41
00:03:16.858 --> 00:03:21.716
The A: two thousand three hundred horsepower, in the Class forty-four.

42
00:03:21.716 --> 00:03:26.712
The B: two thousand five hundred at seven hundred and fifty revolutions,

43
00:03:26.712 --> 00:03:30.529
in Classes forty-five and forty-six, with intercooling.

44
00:03:30.529 --> 00:03:37.539
The C, in the Class forty-seven, was built for two thousand seven hundred and fifty at eight hundred,

45
00:03:37.539 --> 00:03:43.854
and later derated to about two thousand five hundred and eighty at seven hundred and fifty.

46
00:03:45.054 --> 00:03:46.581
Power rises with speed.

47
00:03:46.581 --> 00:03:54.017
The rated points are sourced; the curve between idle and full speed is a modelled shape, and is labelled as one.

48
00:03:54.017 --> 00:03:58.266
As the driver opens the controller, the engine climbs this line.

49
00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:05.852
The driver's power controller has positions off, on, quarter, half, three quarters and full.

50
00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:10.780
The governor turns that demand into fuel, and the engine speed follows.

51
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:15.291
Idle is about three hundred and twenty-five revolutions a minute.

52
00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:22.969
At the rail, a Class forty-seven gives sixty-two thousand pounds of tractive effort at the start,

53
00:04:22.969 --> 00:04:27.376
and thirty thousand pounds continuous at twenty-six miles an hour.

54
00:04:27.376 --> 00:04:31.316
Roughly four-fifths of the engine's power reaches the rail.

55
00:04:31.316 --> 00:04:33.186
That figure is our estimate.

56
00:04:34.386 --> 00:04:39.736
From fuel to rail: engine, generator, six traction motors, gears and wheels.

57
00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:45.439
An electric transmission, which is why one diesel engine can start a heavy train.

58
00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:53.119
Every figure on the site carries a badge: well sourced, single source, weak source, or estimate.

59
00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:57.169
The best sources were the British Railways driver's manuals.

60
00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:04.391
The gaps, such as valve timing, firing order, compression ratio and peak pressure, are marked as estimates.

61
00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.119
Start it, slow it down, pull it apart, and listen.

62
00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:12.224
It is all on the Sulzer page of the almanac.

63
00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:13.636
Thanks for watching.
